# MONKCRAFT VISUAL DESIGN RULE

## Primary reference
The primary visual reference is the "Hustlemad Website Re-Design (Corporate Gifting) 2023" on Behance, especially the provided screenshots reviewed for this project.

The goal is NOT to copy layouts blindly, but to build an original Monkcraft visual system with the same level of polish, colourfulness, playfulness, illustration-led design, and premium creative direction.

## Overall creative direction
Design the website to feel:
- colourful
- playful
- premium
- clean
- aesthetic
- artistic
- cartoon-illustrated
- pictorial
- modern
- soft and rounded
- highly polished
- easy to navigate

Avoid:
- boring corporate visuals
- generic SaaS layouts
- stock-photo-heavy sections
- random AI-looking imagery
- overly serious enterprise styling
- cluttered ecommerce grids
- harsh, boxy, or sterile design
- childish low-quality cartoon styling

## Layout and composition
- Use clean section-based storytelling.
- Each homepage section should feel intentionally art-directed, like a mini poster or designed composition.
- Use generous whitespace and breathable layouts.
- Use asymmetry where helpful, but preserve clarity.
- Use soft rounded containers, layered cards, floating decorative elements, and playful compositions.
- Prioritize clean navigation and easy scanning.

## Background and colour treatment
Use soft pastel and cheerful brand-led colours inspired by the reference style:
- light sky blue
- mint / aqua
- lemon yellow
- coral / peach
- lavender / purple
- cream / off-white
- dark navy / charcoal for grounding text and buttons

Rules:
- background colours should feel soft and uplifting
- sections may use different colour themes while still feeling coherent
- avoid dull grey-heavy layouts
- avoid overusing dark backgrounds
- use colour blocking intentionally

## Typography
- Headlines should feel bold, expressive, and memorable.
- Support text should remain clean and readable.
- Create strong hierarchy between headline, subheadline, body text, and button text.
- Typography should contribute to the premium playful identity.
- Avoid overly technical, cold, or generic typography choices.

## Illustration and decorative style
- Use illustration-led design language where appropriate.
- Decorative visual elements may include:
  - playful blobs
  - soft shapes
  - floating icons
  - illustrated objects
  - 3D/cartoon-style character or object treatment where suitable
- Decorative artwork should feel intentional and high quality.
- Do not use random AI-generated filler art.
- If no approved final illustration assets exist yet, build sections so that illustrations can be added cleanly later.

## Product imagery rules
- Use only real Monkcraft product images for product displays.
- Never fabricate product photos.
- Product images should remain clean, visible, and respected.
- Product sections should be visually rich but not so decorative that product clarity suffers.
- Product cards should stay cleaner than marketing sections.

## UI style
- Rounded buttons
- Soft shadows
- Smooth spacing
- Refined hover states
- Clean cards
- Elegant filter controls
- Well-structured product grids
- Premium but friendly interaction design

## Page-specific visual intent

### Landing page
- Strong illustrated hero with minimal but powerful copy
- clean brand logo strip
- simple “what we do” section
- category/gift idea section
- FAQ
- testimonials
- strong CTA for “Book a Call” and personalized gifting support

### Catalogue
- clean but visually designed background system
- strong filters and category navigation
- 3 products per row where appropriate
- neat product cards with consistent sizing
- product detail interactions must remain clean and readable

### Studio / Catalogue Builder
- professional and clean
- creative but more tool-like than the marketing homepage
- still aligned with the same visual language
- must feel polished and trustworthy

### Company Store
- premium, clean, modern
- friendly and easy for employee use
- should feel branded but not overly playful

## UX priority
No visual choice should make discovery harder.

The customer journey must remain:
- smooth
- effortless
- clear
- trustworthy
- conversion-friendly

Always prefer clarity when a decorative choice hurts usability.

## Implementation behaviour
When generating UI:
- explain how the section matches the visual rule
- preserve consistency with prior approved work
- do not invent missing product or brand assets
- if illustration assets are needed and not yet available, clearly identify placeholders vs production-ready assets---
